<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mangaia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mangaia is the southernmost of the Cook Islands, an ancient volcanic island ringed by a raised coral cliff called the makatea. It had a warlike traditional society ruled by a high priest and victorious war leaders. Christian missionaries arrived in the 1820s, and it joined the Cook Islands federal government in the 1890s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mangaia.jpg" alt="Mangaia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mangaia_(Correct_Orientation).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mangaia, traditionally A'ua'u Enua, meaning terraced island, is the second largest of the Cook Islands after Rarotonga, 203 kilometres from it, and has an area of 51.8 square kilometres. Geologists estimate that it is at least 18 million years old. A fringing coral reef surrounds a high ring of fossil coral cliffs, 60 metres high, called the makatea. The inner rim of the makatea falls steeply to swamps and a central volcanic plateau. The interior drains through underground channels in the makatea, which lead to extensive caves that have been used as refuges and for burials. The highest point is Rangi-motia, 169 metres above sea level. Tamaeu, who came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aitutaki">Aitutaki</a> in 1775, named the island Mangaia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavation of the Tangatatau Rockshelter shows that Mangaia was first settled around 1000 CE; the site also holds the earliest recorded evidence of sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> cultivation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. According to oral tradition, the island was not discovered but emerged from the underworld of Avaiki already populated, and it was later settled further from Rarotonga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aitutaki">Aitutaki</a>, Atiu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> and Tahiti. Adze heads and basalt tools show trade with Rarotonga, Samoa and Raiatea. By the sixteenth century settlement had moved inland from the coast and inter-island trade had virtually disappeared, and oral histories tell of chiefs competing for land and status.</p><p>Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> came on 29 March 1777, the first European visitor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-williams-missionary">John Williams</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> arrived in 1823 but could not land; in 1824 he returned and left two preachers from Taha'a, who began converting the island. Before then the society was marked by violent struggles between war-leaders competing for the title of mangaia, the supreme temporal power, while the ariki served as priests. The last mangaia, Pangemiro, died soon after the missionaries came, and the abolition of war froze the division of land and titles as it was in 1823. In 1888 Mangaia became a British protectorate, and in 1901 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> annexed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mangaia is divided into six districts, with its capital at the village of Oneroa on the west coast, which holds about half the population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-williams-missionary">John Williams</a> estimated the population at two to three thousand in 1823; it peaked at 2,002 in 1966 and had fallen to 499 by 2016. Unlike other southern Cook Islands, Mangaia has kept its collective <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a>, with land use decided by each district's kavana, and its own dialect of Cook Islands Maori, reo Mangaia. It is an Important Bird Area.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mangaia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The New Zealand politician Frederick Moss, appointed British Resident in 1891, persuaded the chiefs to form a federal government in the early 1890s, bringing the Kingdom of Rarotonga, Aitutaki, <b>Mangaia</b> and other islands under one parliament.</blockquote><a href="/state/cook-islands-federation">Cook Islands Federation</a></li></ul></section>
